You can save the extra read in `createPayment`. PutItem accepts `ReturnValuesOnConditionCheckFailure = ALL_OLD`: when the condition fails, the exception carries the item that made it fail, and AWS charges no read units for it. With the raw client:

```kotlin
try {
    low.putItem { it.tableName(table).item(claimItem)
        .conditionExpression("attribute_not_exists(pk)")
        .returnValuesOnConditionCheckFailure(ReturnValuesOnConditionCheckFailure.ALL_OLD) }
} catch (e: ConditionalCheckFailedException) {
    val existing = e.item()   // the claim that won, from the same round trip
}
```

DynamoDB Local 3.3.2 returns the item here too. The lab keeps the separate consistent read because it's easier to follow.

Extra indexes come in two kinds. A local secondary index (LSI) keeps the table's partition key and adds a different sort key, so it's another ordering of the same item collection. You can only create LSIs together with the table, at most five, and a table with LSIs caps each item collection at 10 GB. A global secondary index (GSI) has its own partition key, can be added at any time, and is updated a moment after the table, which is why its reads may be a moment behind. Since November 2025, a GSI key can be made of several attributes, up to four for the partition key and four for the sort key, so you no longer have to glue `status#createdAt` into one string. The next lesson builds the payments GSIs.

TTL deletions show up in DynamoDB Streams, the feed of changes a table can publish, marked as deletions by the service itself. So a program reading the stream can tell an expired claim from one your own code removed.
